# PFQL Plugin System (PLG) Guide

## Overview

**Every data type and schema type in PsiForceDB can be compiled into a PLG plugin**, enabling:

- **Hot-swapping** - Replace plugins without restarting the server
- **Dynamic loading** - Load plugins on-demand
- **Performance tuning** - Speed up or slow down plugins for optimization
- **Custom extensions** - Add your own data types and schema types
- **Modular architecture** - Mix and match plugins for your needs

## Core Concepts

### Plugin Types

1. **Data Type Plugins** - Extend the data type system
2. **Schema Type Plugins** - Extend the schema type system
3. **Operator Plugins** - Custom operators
4. **Function Plugins** - Custom functions
5. **Index Plugins** - Custom indexing strategies
6. **Auth Plugins** - Custom authentication
7. **Custom Plugins** - Your own extensions

### Performance Profiles

- **ULTRA_FAST** - Maximum performance, minimal safety checks
- **FAST** - High performance, standard safety
- **BALANCED** - Balanced performance and safety (default)
- **SAFE** - Maximum safety, standard performance
- **DEBUG** - Debug mode, slow but detailed logging

## Basic Usage

### Loading a Plugin

```pfql
-- Load a plugin
LOAD PLUGIN "plugins/vector_type.plg";

-- Load with options
LOAD PLUGIN "plugins/custom_schema.plg" 
WITH (
    performance_profile = "ultra_fast",
    hot_swappable = true
);
```

### Listing Plugins

```pfql
-- List all loaded plugins
SHOW PLUGINS;

-- List plugins by type
SHOW PLUGINS TYPE data_type;

-- Get plugin details
DESCRIBE PLUGIN "vector_type";
```

### Hot-Swapping Plugins

```pfql
-- Hot-swap a plugin (no restart needed)
HOT_SWAP PLUGIN "vector_type" 
WITH "plugins/vector_type_v2.plg";

-- This updates the plugin while the server is running
-- Active queries continue using the old version until complete
-- New queries use the new version
```

### Performance Tuning

```pfql
-- Speed up a plugin (ultra_fast mode)
SET PLUGIN PERFORMANCE "vector_type" TO ultra_fast;

-- Slow down for safety (debug mode)
SET PLUGIN PERFORMANCE "vector_type" TO debug;

-- Check current performance profile
SHOW PLUGIN PERFORMANCE "vector_type";
```

### Enabling/Disabling Plugins

```pfql
-- Disable a plugin (keeps it loaded but inactive)
DISABLE PLUGIN "experimental_type";

-- Re-enable it
ENABLE PLUGIN "experimental_type";

-- Unload completely
UNLOAD PLUGIN "old_type";
```

## Creating Data Type Plugins

### Step 1: Create Plugin Source

```cpp
// my_custom_type.cpp
#include "PFQL_Plugin_System.hpp"

using namespace PsiForceDB::PFQL::Plugins;

// Plugin metadata
PluginMetadata metadata = {
    .plugin_id = "my_custom_type",
    .plugin_name = "My Custom Type",
    .version = "1.0.0",
    .author = "Your Name",
    .description = "Custom data type for special needs",
    .plugin_type = PluginType::DATA_TYPE,
    .is_hot_swappable = true,
    .performance_profile = PerformanceProfile::BALANCED
};

// Plugin interface
DataTypePluginInterface interface;

// Initialize function
bool initialize() {
    // Setup your data type
    interface.parse_type = [](const std::string& type_str) -> DataType {
        if (type_str == "MY_CUSTOM_TYPE") {
            return DataType::CUSTOM;
        }
        return DataType::STRING;
    };

    interface.validate_value = [](const std::string& value, DataType type) -> bool {
        // Your validation logic
        return true;
    };

    interface.set_performance_profile = [](PerformanceProfile profile) {
        // Adjust performance based on profile
    };

    return true;
}

// Shutdown function
void shutdown() {
    // Cleanup
}

// Reload function (for hot-swap)
bool reload() {
    // Reload configuration or state
    return true;
}

// Export plugin
PFQL_EXPORT_PLUGIN(DATA_TYPE, interface)
PFQL_PLUGIN_ENTRY_POINT
```

### Step 2: Compile Plugin

```bash
# Compile to PLG file
g++ -shared -fPIC -o my_custom_type.plg my_custom_type.cpp \
    -I/path/to/psiforcedb/include \
    -L/path/to/psiforcedb/lib -lpsiforcedb
```

### Step 3: Load and Use

```pfql
-- Load your plugin
LOAD PLUGIN "my_custom_type.plg";

-- Use your custom type
CREATE TABLE test (
    id INT,
    data MY_CUSTOM_TYPE
);

-- The plugin handles all operations
INSERT INTO test VALUES (1, 'custom_value');
SELECT * FROM test;
```

