-- For production, use ultra_fast
SET PLUGIN PERFORMANCE "vector_type" TO ultra_fast;

-- This disables some safety checks for maximum speed
-- Use only with trusted data
```

### Debug Mode

```pfql
-- For debugging, use debug mode
SET PLUGIN PERFORMANCE "vector_type" TO debug;

-- Enables detailed logging and validation
-- Slower but safer
```

### Per-Query Performance

```pfql
-- Set performance for specific query
SELECT * FROM vectors 
WITH PLUGIN_PERFORMANCE("vector_type", "ultra_fast")
WHERE vector <=> query_vector < 0.5;
```

## Hot-Swapping Workflow

### Zero-Downtime Updates

```pfql
-- 1. Load new version alongside old
LOAD PLUGIN "vector_type_v2.plg" AS "vector_type_new";

-- 2. Test new version
TEST PLUGIN "vector_type_new";

-- 3. Hot-swap when ready
HOT_SWAP PLUGIN "vector_type" WITH "vector_type_new";

-- 4. Old version unloads automatically
-- No queries are interrupted
```

### Rolling Updates

```pfql
-- Update plugin across cluster
HOT_SWAP PLUGIN "vector_type" 
WITH "vector_type_v2.plg" 
ON ALL NODES;
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Plugin Design

- **Keep plugins focused** - One plugin, one purpose
- **Make them hot-swappable** - Design for zero-downtime updates
- **Version everything** - Track versions for compatibility
- **Document thoroughly** - Include usage examples

### 2. Performance

- **Use appropriate profiles** - Don't always use ultra_fast
- **Profile your plugins** - Measure before optimizing
- **Cache when possible** - Pure functions can be cached
- **Lazy initialization** - Don't do heavy work at load time

### 3. Security

- **Sandbox untrusted plugins** - Isolate third-party code
- **Validate inputs** - Never trust plugin data
- **Limit permissions** - Plugins shouldn't access everything
- **Audit plugin usage** - Log what plugins do

### 4. Maintenance

- **Test before deploying** - Use test environment
- **Monitor plugin health** - Track errors and performance
- **Keep plugins updated** - Security patches matter
- **Document changes** - Changelogs help users

## Plugin Template Generator

```pfql
-- Generate plugin template
GENERATE PLUGIN TEMPLATE 
TYPE data_type 
NAME "my_type" 
OUTPUT "my_type.cpp";

-- This creates a ready-to-customize plugin source file
```

## Troubleshooting

### Plugin Won't Load

```pfql
-- Check plugin status
SHOW PLUGIN STATUS "my_plugin";

-- Check for errors
SHOW PLUGIN ERRORS "my_plugin";

-- Verify compatibility
CHECK PLUGIN COMPATIBILITY "my_plugin";
```

### Performance Issues

```pfql
-- Profile plugin
PROFILE PLUGIN "my_plugin";

-- Check resource usage
SHOW PLUGIN RESOURCES "my_plugin";

-- Adjust performance profile
SET PLUGIN PERFORMANCE "my_plugin" TO balanced;
```

## Conclusion

The PLG plugin system makes PsiForceDB **infinitely extensible**:

- **Compile any data type** to a plugin
- **Hot-swap without downtime**
- **Tune performance** for your needs
- **Add custom functionality** easily
- **Maintain modularity** and flexibility

**Build. Load. Swap. Optimize. Extend without limits.**

# PFQL vs SQL: Complete Syntax Comparison

## Overview

**PFQL (PsiForce Query Language)** has TWO distinct components:

1. **PFQL Protocol** - Ergonomic command system with protocol prefixes
2. **PFQL Query Language** - Database query syntax (SQL-like but enhanced)

---

## PART 1: PFQL PROTOCOL (Ergonomic Command System)

### Source Code Location
- **Header**: `include/pfql_real_processor.hpp`
- **Implementation**: Inline in header (methods defined inline)
- **Parser**: `PFQL_Command_Parser` class

### PFQL Protocol Syntax

```
[protocol]://[namespace]::[command]; [@target] [arguments] [--options]
```

### Protocols Available

| Protocol | Description | Example |
|----------|-------------|---------|
| `pf://` | PsiForceDB protocol (default) | `pf://db::query; SELECT * FROM users` |
| `ms://` | MedusaServ protocol | `ms://server::restart;` |
| `iw://` | Innocuous Access protocol | `iw://ai::connect; @10.0.0.4` |
| `ssh://` | SSH execution | `ssh://system::exec; ls -la` |
| `cmd://` | Windows CMD | `cmd://system::dir;` |
| `local://` | Local execution | `local://script::run;` |

### PFQL Protocol Examples

#### Example 1: Database Query
```pfql
pf://db::query; SELECT * FROM users WHERE status = 'active'
```

#### Example 2: Namespace Command
```pfql
pf://admin::create_user; username=john --role=admin --godmode
```

#### Example 3: Remote Target
```pfql
pf://db::backup; @10.0.0.2 --format=sqlite --compress
```

#### Example 4: Multiple Options
```pfql
ms://server::restart; --graceful --wait=30 --notify
```

### PFQL Protocol Structure

```cpp
struct Parsed_Command {
    std::string protocol;              // "pf://", "ms://", etc.
    std::string namespace_name;        // "db", "admin", "system"
    std::string command_name;          // "query", "create_user", etc.
    std::vector<std::string> arguments; // Positional arguments
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> options; // --key=value
    std::string target;                // @hostname or @ip
    bool requires_root = false;         // Needs root privileges
    bool is_remote = false;             // Remote execution
    std::string raw_command;            // Original command string
    std::string error;                  // Parse error if any
};
```

---

## PART 2: PFQL QUERY LANGUAGE vs SQL

### Source Code Location
- **Engine**: `include/database_engine.hpp`
- **Class**: `PsiForceDB::Database::PsiForceEngine`
- **Method**: `execute(const std::string& command)`

### Side-by-Side Comparison

| Operation | SQL Syntax | PFQL Syntax | Notes |
|-----------|------------|-------------|-------|
| **CREATE TABLE** | `CREATE TABLE users (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(255))` | `CREATE TABLE users (id, name)` | PFQL uses simplified column definitions (all STRING by default) |
| **CREATE TABLE (IF NOT EXISTS)** | `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (...)` | `CREATE TABLE users (...)` | PFQL doesn't support IF NOT EXISTS (engine handles it) |
| **INSERT** | `INSERT INTO users VALUES (1, 'John')` | `INSERT INTO users VALUES ('1', 'John')` | PFQL treats all values as strings |
| **INSERT (with columns)** | `INSERT INTO users (id, name) VALUES (1, 'John')` | `INSERT INTO users VALUES ('1', 'John')` | PFQL uses positional matching |
| **SELECT** | `SELECT * FROM users` | `SELECT * FROM users` | ✅ Identical |
| **SELECT (with WHERE)** | `SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1` | `SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '1'` | PFQL requires string quotes |
| **SELECT (with JOIN)** | `SELECT u.name, o.total FROM users u JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id` | `SELECT * FROM users` | ⚠️ PFQL currently doesn't support JOINs |
| **DROP TABLE** | `DROP TABLE users` | `DROP TABLE users` | ✅ Identical |
| **SHOW TABLES** | `SHOW TABLES` | `SHOW TABLES` | ✅ Identical |
| **SNAPSHOT** | `CREATE SNAPSHOT backup_2025` | `SNAPSHOT backup_2025` | PFQL uses simplified syntax |
| **ROLLBACK** | `ROLLBACK TO SNAPSHOT backup_2025` | `ROLLBACK backup_2025` | PFQL uses simplified syntax |

### PFQL Query Language Examples

#### Example 1: Create Table
```sql
-- SQL
CREATE TABLE component_reviews (
    component_id VARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,
    component_name VARCHAR(255),
    current_status VARCHAR(50),
    total_reviews INT,
    licensing_verified BOOLEAN
);
```

```pfql
-- PFQL
CREATE TABLE component_reviews (
    component_id, component_name, current_status,
    total_reviews, licensing_verified
)
```

#### Example 2: Insert Data
```sql
-- SQL
INSERT INTO component_reviews
VALUES ('ext_001', 'MyExtension', 'GREEN', 5, true);
```

```pfql
-- PFQL
INSERT INTO component_reviews
VALUES ('ext_001', 'MyExtension', 'GREEN', '5', 'true')
```

#### Example 3: Select Data
```sql
-- SQL
SELECT * FROM component_reviews
WHERE current_status = 'GREEN' AND total_reviews > 3;
```

```pfql
-- PFQL
SELECT * FROM component_reviews
-- Note: WHERE clause parsing not fully implemented yet
```

#### Example 4: Combined PFQL Protocol + Query
```pfql
pf://db::execute; CREATE TABLE users (id, name, email)
```

```pfql
pf://db::query; SELECT * FROM users
```

---

## PART 3: PFQL Engine Implementation

### Current Implementation Status

**Supported Commands:**
- ✅ `CREATE TABLE` - Creates tables with column names
- ✅ `INSERT INTO ... VALUES` - Inserts rows
- ✅ `SELECT * FROM table` - Selects all rows (returns formatted string)
- ✅ `DROP TABLE` - Drops tables
- ✅ `SHOW TABLES` - Lists all tables
- ✅ `SNAPSHOT` - Creates database snapshots
- ✅ `ROLLBACK` - Rolls back to snapshot

**Not Yet Implemented:**
- ❌ `WHERE` clause filtering
- ❌ `JOIN` operations
- ❌ `GROUP BY`, `HAVING`, `ORDER BY`
- ❌ `UPDATE`, `DELETE`
- ❌ Data type specifications (all columns are STRING)
- ❌ `PRIMARY KEY`, `FOREIGN KEY` constraints
- ❌ Indexes

### Query Method

The engine provides two methods:

```cpp
// Execute command and get string result
std::string execute(const std::string& command);

// Query and get structured results (NEW - added for Peer Review System)
std::vector<std::vector<std::string>> query(const std::string& pfql_command);
```

**Example Usage:**
```cpp
auto engine = std::make_unique<PsiForceDB::Database::PsiForceEngine>(
    db_path, version_control);

// Execute (returns formatted string)
std::string result = engine->execute("SELECT * FROM users");
// Result: "id\tname\temail\n1\tJohn\tjohn@example.com\n"

// Query (returns structured data)
auto rows = engine->query("SELECT * FROM users");
// Result: {{"1", "John", "john@example.com"}, {"2", "Jane", "jane@example.com"}}
```

---

## PART 4: Ergonomic PFQL Source Code

### Parser Implementation

**File**: `include/pfql_real_processor.hpp`

```cpp
class PFQL_Command_Parser {
public:
    struct Parsed_Command {
        std::string protocol;              // "pf://", "ms://", etc.
        std::string namespace_name;        // Optional namespace
        std::string command_name;          // Command to execute
        std::vector<std::string> arguments; // Positional args
        std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> options; // --key=value
        std::string target;                // @hostname
        bool requires_root = false;
        bool is_remote = false;
        std::string raw_command;
        std::string error;
    };

    std::unique_ptr<Parsed_Command> parse(const std::string& command_str);

private:
    std::regex protocol_pattern;    // Matches "pf://", "ms://", etc.
    std::regex namespace_pattern;   // Matches namespace names
    std::regex command_pattern;     // Matches command names
    std::regex target_pattern;      // Matches @target
};
```

### Protocol Parsing Logic

```cpp
// 1. Parse protocol (pf://, ms://, etc.)
if (std::regex_search(working_str, protocol_match, protocol_pattern)) {
    result->protocol = protocol_match[0];
    working_str = working_str.substr(result->protocol.length());
} else {
    result->protocol = "pf://";  // Default
}

// 2. Parse namespace (namespace::)
size_t namespace_pos = working_str.find("::");
if (namespace_pos != std::string::npos) {
    result->namespace_name = working_str.substr(0, namespace_pos);
    working_str = working_str.substr(namespace_pos + 2);
}

// 3. Parse command (command;)
size_t command_pos = working_str.find(";");
if (command_pos != std::string::npos) {
    result->command_name = working_str.substr(0, command_pos);
    working_str = working_str.substr(command_pos + 1);
}

// 4. Parse target (@hostname)
std::smatch target_match;
if (std::regex_search(working_str, target_match, target_pattern)) {
    result->target = target_match[1];
    result->is_remote = true;
    working_str = std::regex_replace(working_str, target_pattern, "");
}

// 5. Parse options (--key=value or --flag)
// ... option parsing logic ...
```

---

## PART 5: Real-World Usage Examples

### Example 1: Peer Review System (Current Implementation)

```cpp
