# LFSSL Dual Module System

## Philosophy
**"Two Ends of the Scale"**

The foundation remains the same.
Modules provide backwards compatibility AND forward-looking modern crypto.

## Architecture

```
LFSSL Foundation (Immutable)
├── ICryptoProvider Interface (Never Changes)
│
├── Legacy Module (Backwards Compatible)
│   ├── TLS 1.0, 1.1, < 1.2
│   ├── Older cipher suites
│   ├── Legacy protocol support
│   └── For connecting to old systems
│
└── Bleeding_Edge Module (Cutting-Edge)
    ├── TLS 1.3 (Our Standard)
    ├── AES-256 (Our Standard)
    ├── Latest cipher suites
    ├── Post-quantum ready
    └── For cutting-edge systems
```

## Module Types

### Legacy Module
- **Purpose:** Backwards compatibility
- **Protocols:** TLS 1.0, 1.1, < 1.2
- **Ciphers:** Older, but still secure
- **Use Case:** Connect to legacy systems
- **Example:** `LegacyCryptoModule`

### Bleeding_Edge Module
- **Purpose:** Cutting-edge, latest standards
- **Protocols:** TLS 1.3 (minimum)
- **Ciphers:** AES-256 (our standard)
- **Use Case:** All new connections, latest tech
- **Example:** `BleedingEdgeCryptoModule`

## Foundation Interface (Immutable)

```cpp
namespace LFSSL {
namespace Core {

class ICryptoProvider {
    // Interface never changes
    // Both modules implement this
};

} // namespace Core
} // namespace LFSSL
```

## Module Selection

```cpp
// Get module based on context
auto* legacy = registry.getModule("Legacy");   // For old systems
auto* modern = registry.getModule("Modern");   // For new systems

// Or auto-select based on connection
if (client_supports_tls13) {
    useModule("Modern");
} else {
    useModule("Legacy");  // Fallback to legacy
}
```

## Example: TLS Handshake

```cpp
// Bleeding_Edge connection (TLS 1.3)
auto* bleeding_edge = registry.getModule("Bleeding_Edge");
bleeding_edge->negotiateTLS(version::TLS_1_3, cipher::AES_256_GCM);

// Legacy connection (TLS 1.1)
auto* legacy = registry.getModule("Legacy");
legacy->negotiateTLS(version::TLS_1_1, cipher::AES_128_CBC);
```

## Benefits

1. **Foundation Never Changes** - Interface remains constant
2. **Backwards Compatible** - Can talk to old systems
3. **Forward Looking** - Modern standards (AES-256, TLS 1.3)
4. **Swappable** - Choose module based on context
5. **Coexistence** - Both modules can be loaded simultaneously

## Implementation

```cpp
// Legacy Module
class LegacyCryptoModule : public ICryptoProvider {
    // Supports TLS < 1.2
    // Older ciphers
    // Backwards compatible
};

// Modern Module
class ModernCryptoModule : public ICryptoProvider {
    // Supports TLS 1.3+
    // AES-256 (our standard)
    // Modern crypto
};
```

## Key Insight

**"The base already remains the same BUT the other modules are the replaceable part"**

- Foundation = Immutable
- Legacy Module = Replaceable (for old systems)
- Modern Module = Replaceable (for new systems)
- **AES-256 is our standard** (in the modern module)

# LFSSL Development Standards

## Gold Standards - Non-Negotiable

**"Zero Stubs, Zero Forward Declarations, No Accepted Errors, No Accepted Warnings"**

These standards are **MANDATORY** for all LFSSL development. They align with the Back-to-Front (B2F) Development Policy - the foundation must be correct from the start.

---

## 🔴 STANDARD 1: ZERO STUBS

### Rule
**NO stub implementations allowed. All code must be fully implemented.**

### What is a Stub?
- Empty function bodies (`{}`)
- Functions that only return default values
- Functions with `// TODO` or `// FIXME` comments
- Functions that throw "not implemented" exceptions
- Placeholder implementations

### ✅ CORRECT
```cpp
// Full implementation
std::vector<uint8_t> SHA256::hash(const uint8_t* data, size_t length) {
    SHA256 hasher;
    hasher.update(data, length);
    return hasher.finalize();
}
```

### ❌ WRONG (Stub)
```cpp
// STUB - NOT ALLOWED
std::vector<uint8_t> SHA256::hash(const uint8_t* data, size_t length) {
    // TODO: Implement
    return std::vector<uint8_t>();
}
```

### Enforcement
- [ ] Code review must verify no stubs
- [ ] Automated tests must verify all functions work
- [ ] Compilation must succeed with full implementations
- [ ] All functions must have real, working code

---

## 🔴 STANDARD 2: ZERO FORWARD DECLARATIONS

### Rule
**NO forward declarations in headers. All dependencies must be fully included.**

### What is a Forward Declaration?
- `class SomeClass;` (without implementation)
- `struct SomeStruct;` (without definition)
- `void someFunction();` (without implementation)

### ✅ CORRECT
```cpp
// Full include
#include "lfssl/LFSSL_Native_Crypto.hpp"
#include "lfssl/security/audit_logger.hpp"

class MyClass {
    LFSSL::Crypto::SHA256 hasher;
    LFSSL::Security::AuditLogger logger;
};
```

### ❌ WRONG (Forward Declaration)
```cpp
// Forward declaration - NOT ALLOWED
class SHA256;
class AuditLogger;

class MyClass {
    SHA256* hasher;  // Pointer because of forward declaration
    AuditLogger* logger;
};
```

### Enforcement
- [ ] All headers must include full definitions
- [ ] No pointer workarounds for forward declarations
- [ ] All dependencies must be explicit includes
- [ ] Header files must be self-contained

---

## 🔴 STANDARD 3: NO ACCEPTED ERRORS

### Rule
**ZERO compilation errors allowed. Code must compile cleanly.**

### What is an Error?
- Compilation errors (syntax, type mismatches, missing symbols)
- Link errors (undefined references, missing libraries)
- Runtime errors (crashes, exceptions from code issues)

### ✅ CORRECT
```cpp
// Compiles without errors
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

namespace LFSSL {
namespace Crypto {
    class SHA256 {
    public:
        std::vector<uint8_t> hash(const uint8_t* data, size_t length);
    };
}
}
```

### ❌ WRONG (Error)
```cpp
// ERROR - Missing include
class SHA256 {
public:
    std::vector<uint8_t> hash(const uint8_t* data, size_t length);  // ERROR: std::vector not defined
};
```

### Enforcement
- [ ] Code must compile with zero errors
- [ ] All includes must be present
- [ ] All types must be defined
- [ ] All symbols must be resolved
- [ ] No "we'll fix it later" - fix it NOW

---

## 🔴 STANDARD 4: NO ACCEPTED WARNINGS

### Rule
**ZERO compiler warnings allowed. Code must compile with clean output.**

### What is a Warning?
- Unused variables
- Type conversions
- Deprecated function usage
- Missing return statements
- Uninitialized variables
- Any compiler warning message

### ✅ CORRECT
```cpp
// No warnings
void processData(const uint8_t* data, size_t length) {
    if (data == nullptr || length == 0) {
        return;  // Explicit return
    }

    // Use all variables
    size_t processed = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
        processByte(data[i]);
        processed++;
    }

    // Variable is used
    logProcessed(processed);
}
```

### ❌ WRONG (Warning)
```cpp
// WARNING - Unused variable
void processData(const uint8_t* data, size_t length) {
    size_t processed = 0;  // WARNING: Unused variable
    // ... code that doesn't use 'processed'
}
```

### Compiler Flags
```cmake
# MSVC
set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} /W4 /WX")  # All warnings, treat as errors

# GCC/Clang
set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror")
```

### Enforcement
- [ ] Compiler must output zero warnings
- [ ] All warnings must be fixed, not suppressed
- [ ] No `#pragma warning(disable: ...)` without fixing the issue
- [ ] Code must be warning-free from the start

---

## 🔴 STANDARD 5: CPP AND HPP MUST MATCH

### Rule
**Header (.hpp) and implementation (.cpp) files must be perfectly synchronized.**

### Matching Requirements

#### 1. Function Signatures Must Match Exactly
```cpp
// header.hpp
class MyClass {
public:
    std::vector<uint8_t> process(const uint8_t* data, size_t length);
};

// implementation.cpp
// ✅ CORRECT - Exact match
std::vector<uint8_t> MyClass::process(const uint8_t* data, size_t length) {
    // Implementation
}

// ❌ WRONG - Mismatch
std::vector<uint8_t> MyClass::process(const uint8_t* data) {  // Missing 'length' parameter
    // Implementation
}
```

#### 2. Namespace Must Match
```cpp
// header.hpp
namespace LFSSL {
namespace Crypto {
    class SHA256 { };
}
}

// implementation.cpp
// ✅ CORRECT - Exact match
namespace LFSSL {
namespace Crypto {
    SHA256::SHA256() { }
}
}

// ❌ WRONG - Mismatch
namespace LFSSL {  // Missing Crypto namespace
    SHA256::SHA256() { }
}
```

#### 3. Class Name Must Match
```cpp
// header.hpp
class SHA256 { };

// implementation.cpp
// ✅ CORRECT
SHA256::SHA256() { }

// ❌ WRONG
SHA256Impl::SHA256Impl() { }  // Different class name
```

#### 4. All Declared Functions Must Be Implemented
```cpp
// header.hpp
class MyClass {
public:
    void function1();
    void function2();
    void function3();
};

// implementation.cpp
// ✅ CORRECT - All implemented
void MyClass::function1() { }
void MyClass::function2() { }
void MyClass::function3() { }

// ❌ WRONG - Missing implementation
void MyClass::function1() { }
void MyClass::function2() { }
// function3() is missing!
```

#### 5. All Implemented Functions Must Be Declared
```cpp
// header.hpp
class MyClass {
public:
    void function1();
};

// implementation.cpp
// ❌ WRONG - Extra implementation
void MyClass::function1() { }
void MyClass::function2() { }  // Not declared in header!
```

### Validation Checklist
- [ ] Every function in .hpp has implementation in .cpp
- [ ] Every function in .cpp is declared in .hpp
- [ ] Function signatures match exactly (parameters, return type, const)
- [ ] Namespace matches exactly
- [ ] Class name matches exactly
- [ ] No orphaned declarations
- [ ] No orphaned implementations

