# GalaxyCDN - Public CDN Protocol Library

**Copyright © 2025 D Hargreaves | Roylepython AKA The Medusa Initiative 2025 - All Rights Reserved**

**Yorkshire Champion Standards - Improving AI Safety and the Web**

## Overview

GalaxyCDN is a high-performance Content Delivery Network protocol library designed for dual-stack IPv4/IPv6 networking. It works seamlessly with the std_execution (Amphisbaena) networking library for complete dual-stack support.

## Features

- **Dual-Stack Support**: Works with IPv4 and IPv6
- **High Performance**: Optimized for low latency and high throughput
- **Protocol Versioning**: Built-in version management
- **Message Types**: Request, Response, Stream, Control, Heartbeat, Error
- **Compression & Encryption**: Protocol flags for compressed/encrypted messages
- **Standalone or Integrated**: Can work alone or with std_execution

## Building

### Ubuntu/WSL Build

```bash
cd "C:\McMaker Projects\Tools\libs\GalaxyCDN"
chmod +x build_wsl_ubuntu.sh
./build_wsl_ubuntu.sh
```

### Manual Build

```bash
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
make -j$(nproc)
```

## Usage

### Standalone Usage

```cpp
#include "galaxycdn/server.h"
#include "galaxycdn/client.h"

// Server
GalaxyCDN::Server server(8080);
server.set_message_handler([](const std::string& client_id, const GalaxyCDN::Message& msg) {
    // Handle message
});
server.start();

// Client
GalaxyCDN::Client client;
client.connect("127.0.0.1", 8080);
GalaxyCDN::Message msg = GalaxyCDN::Protocol::create_message(
    GalaxyCDN::MessageType::REQUEST, 
    payload_data
);
client.send_message(msg);
```

### Integration with std_execution

```cpp
#include "galaxycdn/protocol.h"
#include "network/async_connection_manager.h"

// Use GalaxyCDN protocol with std_execution async connections
auto conn_id = connection_manager->create_async_connection(addr, port);
connection_manager->send_galaxycdn_message(conn_id, payload);
```

## Protocol Specification

- **Magic Number**: 0x47414C58 ("GALX")
- **Version**: 1
- **Max Payload**: 16MB
- **Header Size**: 32 bytes

## Testing

Run tests:
```bash
cd build
ctest --output-on-failure
```

